In a cold‑sprayed metal coating the surface that contacts the counterbody is a lamellar “splatted” microstructure with many particle–particle interfaces that are weaker than the bulk material.  

**Unidirectional sliding** continuously shears these interfaces in the same sense. The repeated forward shear

* builds up a large, monotonic plastic strain in each particle and at every interface;  
* promotes progressive work‑hardening of the near‑surface layer, raising the coefficient of friction (COF);  
* accelerates particle fragmentation, micro‑cracking and delamination of splats, which creates fresh wear debris that acts as a third body;  
* limits the time for any protective oxide film to form or to be retained because the high, steady shear temperature is localized and the film is repeatedly removed by the advancing wear front.

The net result is a higher wear rate (greater mass loss) under unidirectional motion.

**Oscillating sliding** reverses the shear direction each half‑cycle. This reversal

* reduces the net accumulated plastic strain in any given particle, allowing the material to relax elastically between passes;  
* lowers the average COF because the forward and reverse shears partially cancel the build‑up of frictional heating;  
* gives the surface more opportunity for oxidation or formation of a thin, hard oxide layer (e.g., TiO₂ on Ti‑based coatings) that can remain in place for part of the cycle, acting as a protective glaze;  
* limits crack propagation and splat delamination because tensile stresses are reduced and fatigue‑type damage is less severe.

Consequently, oscillating motion produces milder abrasive/adhesive wear and often lower mass loss than continuous unidirectional sliding. The difference is fundamentally a matter of strain accumulation and the ability (or inability) to develop and retain protective surface films during the sliding cycle.
